1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,505
On the eighteenth of October 2025, at a new factory in Lucknow,

2
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:08,504
India's Defence Minister flagged off the first batch of missiles built

3
00:00:08,504 --> 00:00:12,917
there — and said something that travelled further than the missiles did.

4
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:19,606
Every inch of Pakistan's territory, he said, is now within the reach of BrahMos.

5
00:00:19,606 --> 00:00:23,225
And Operation Sindoor, he added, was just a trailer.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:28,402
This is BrahMos: a missile India built with Russia, named after two rivers,

7
00:00:28,402 --> 00:00:32,352
and now the first weapon of its class India has ever sold abroad.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,072
Four questions.

9
00:00:37,072 --> 00:00:38,679
Where did it come from?

10
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:40,354
What can it actually do?

11
00:00:40,354 --> 00:00:42,497
Is there anything else like it?

12
00:00:42,497 --> 00:00:44,271
And who is buying it?

13
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:49,195
Start in Moscow, on the twelfth of February 1998.

14
00:00:49,195 --> 00:00:57,740
Two men sign an inter-governmental agreement: Dr APJ Abdul Kalam, then the head of India's defence research organisation, and N.V.

15
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:01,366
Mikhailov, Russia's First Deputy Defence Minister.

16
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:12,122
The deal creates a joint company, BrahMos Aerospace, owned by India's DRDO and Russia's NPO Mashinostroyeniya.

17
00:01:12,122 --> 00:01:14,100
And the name is the giveaway.

18
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,947
Brah, from the Brahmaputra.

19
00:01:15,947 --> 00:01:17,398
Mos, from the Moskva.

20
00:01:17,398 --> 00:01:20,337
Two rivers, two countries, one missile.

21
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:27,647
India wasn't starting from a blank page, and that's the point.

22
00:01:27,647 --> 00:01:31,642
BrahMos is built on a Russian anti-ship missile, the P-800 Oniks,

23
00:01:31,642 --> 00:01:36,908
with Russian ramjet propulsion married to Indian guidance, software and airframe work.

24
00:01:36,908 --> 00:01:39,690
That shortcut is why it arrived so fast.

25
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:51,412
The first test flight came on the twelfth of June 2001, from a stationary launcher at Chandipur, on the Odisha coast.

26
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:53,449
The Navy inducted it in 2005.

27
00:01:53,449 --> 00:01:55,283
The Army followed in 2007.

28
00:01:55,283 --> 00:02:02,577
And in January 2020, the Air Force commissioned its first squadron of Sukhoi-30s modified to carry it.

29
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:05,252
So what does it actually do?

30
00:02:05,252 --> 00:02:07,592
Three things, and they work together.

31
00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:08,331
One: speed.

32
00:02:08,331 --> 00:02:14,181
BrahMos cruises at close to three times the speed of sound — and here's the part that matters:

33
00:02:14,181 --> 00:02:18,546
it does that for the whole flight, not just the last few seconds.

34
00:02:20,767 --> 00:02:25,756
Against a subsonic missile, a ship's defences might have a couple of minutes to react.

35
00:02:25,756 --> 00:02:28,121
Against BrahMos, they have seconds.

36
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:30,439
Two: it flies low.

37
00:02:30,439 --> 00:02:35,793
In its final run, BrahMos can come down to around ten metres above the sea.

38
00:02:35,793 --> 00:02:37,713
And the sea is curved.

39
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:44,983
A ship's radar looks out in a straight line, so it simply cannot see something that low until it is already close.

40
00:02:44,983 --> 00:02:49,584
By the time the missile appears over the horizon, there is almost no time left.

41
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:54,309
Three: nobody is flying it.

42
00:02:54,309 --> 00:02:57,238
BrahMos is what engineers call fire-and-forget.

43
00:02:57,238 --> 00:03:02,853
Once it leaves the launcher, it navigates by its own inertial system and satellite signals,

44
00:03:02,853 --> 00:03:06,510
then switches on its own radar for the final approach.

45
00:03:09,933 --> 00:03:12,523
The crew that fired it can turn around and leave.

46
00:03:12,523 --> 00:03:15,890
Its makers claim it lands within about a metre of the aim point,

47
00:03:15,890 --> 00:03:20,075
carrying a conventional warhead of two hundred to three hundred kilograms.

48
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:28,930
And it launches from almost anywhere: a truck on a road, a warship's deck, a submarine, or the belly of a fighter.

49
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:31,858
Very few missiles in the world cover all four.

50
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:39,425
Now, range — and this is where politics shaped the engineering.

51
00:03:39,425 --> 00:03:43,445
For years, BrahMos flew about two hundred and ninety kilometres.

52
00:03:43,445 --> 00:03:46,776
Not because of physics, but because of a treaty.

53
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:57,637
The Missile Technology Control Regime restricts exports of missiles that can carry a five-hundred-kilogram payload further than three hundred kilometres,

54
00:03:57,637 --> 00:03:59,113
and Russia is a member.

55
00:03:59,113 --> 00:04:00,036
India was not.

56
00:04:00,036 --> 00:04:03,226
In 2016, India joined, and the ceiling lifted.

57
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:09,794
Longer versions followed, tested to around four hundred and fifty kilometres,

58
00:04:09,794 --> 00:04:13,625
and an eight-hundred-kilometre version has been reported in trials.

59
00:04:13,625 --> 00:04:17,118
Export customers still get the three-hundred-kilometre class.

60
00:04:17,118 --> 00:04:18,640
The treaty didn't go away.

61
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:21,306
India just moved to the other side of it.

62
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:25,689
So, is there anything like it?

63
00:04:25,689 --> 00:04:26,886
Let's line them up.

64
00:04:26,886 --> 00:04:30,955
America's Tomahawk flies between one thousand two hundred and fifty

65
00:04:30,955 --> 00:04:35,085
and two thousand five hundred kilometres — far further than BrahMos.

66
00:04:35,085 --> 00:04:38,557
But it's subsonic, roughly the speed of an airliner.

67
00:04:40,167 --> 00:04:44,586
The Harpoon and France's Exocet are subsonic too, and much shorter-legged.

68
00:04:44,586 --> 00:04:48,474
Russia's Kalibr and China's YJ-18 do something clever in between:

69
00:04:48,474 --> 00:04:53,488
they cruise subsonic to save fuel, then sprint supersonic in the final seconds.

70
00:04:57,233 --> 00:04:58,273
See the pattern?

71
00:04:58,273 --> 00:05:01,453
Almost every cruise missile trades speed for range.

72
00:05:01,453 --> 00:05:03,960
Fuel is finite, and going fast burns it.

73
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:10,130
BrahMos makes the opposite trade: it accepts a shorter reach in order to be fast the whole way.

74
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:13,842
So, unique?

75
00:05:13,842 --> 00:05:14,522
Not quite.

76
00:05:14,522 --> 00:05:19,222
Russia's Zircon is faster still — hypersonic, a different class altogether.

77
00:05:19,222 --> 00:05:24,594
And the Oniks that BrahMos grew from is still a Russian missile in its own right.

78
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:31,311
But supersonic-all-the-way cruise missiles in service anywhere are rare.

79
00:05:31,311 --> 00:05:36,689
And BrahMos is the one that is now built in India, improved in India, and sold by India.

80
00:05:36,689 --> 00:05:39,588
That last part is what changed everything.

81
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,002
BrahMos has a combat record.

82
00:05:44,002 --> 00:05:48,145
It also has an accident record, and both are worth knowing.

83
00:05:48,145 --> 00:05:52,288
On the ninth of March 2022, a BrahMos was fired by mistake.

84
00:05:52,288 --> 00:05:58,157
It flew into Pakistani territory and came down near Mian Channu, in Punjab province.

85
00:05:58,157 --> 00:06:01,495
It carried no warhead, and nobody was hurt.

86
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:09,324
India's Defence Ministry called it an accidental firing caused by a technical malfunction during routine maintenance,

87
00:06:09,324 --> 00:06:11,847
and said it deeply regretted the incident.

88
00:06:11,847 --> 00:06:15,492
In August 2022, three Air Force officers were dismissed.

89
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:22,538
Pakistan called India's explanation inadequate and asked for a joint investigation, which did not happen.

90
00:06:22,538 --> 00:06:29,266
It remains the only publicly known case of one nuclear-armed state accidentally firing a missile into another.

91
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,685
Then, in May 2025, came Operation Sindoor.

92
00:06:33,685 --> 00:06:38,851
India struck targets in Pakistan on the night of the ninth and tenth of May.

93
00:06:38,851 --> 00:06:40,627
Now, be careful here.

94
00:06:41,533 --> 00:06:45,504
The Defence Ministry has not published a detailed list of the weapons used,

95
00:06:45,504 --> 00:06:52,020
and the reports you may have seen naming exact numbers of missiles and airbases are media accounts, not official ones.

96
00:06:55,067 --> 00:07:00,334
What is on the record is this: five months later, standing inside the BrahMos factory at Lucknow,

97
00:07:00,334 --> 00:07:07,782
the Defence Minister said that every inch of Pakistan is within the missile's reach, and that the operation had been just a trailer.

98
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:10,789
Read that how you like.

99
00:07:10,789 --> 00:07:15,602
It is an unusually direct statement from a government about one of its own weapons.

100
00:07:17,567 --> 00:07:19,396
Which brings us to the sales.

101
00:07:19,396 --> 00:07:22,140
For twenty years, BrahMos was India's alone.

102
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:24,274
That changed with the Philippines.

103
00:07:24,274 --> 00:07:29,701
Manila signed in January 2022, for about three hundred and seventy-five million dollars:

104
00:07:29,701 --> 00:07:33,293
shore-based anti-ship batteries for its Marine Corps.

105
00:07:34,267 --> 00:07:42,451
The first batch arrived on the nineteenth of April 2024 — the first time India had exported a major missile system to anybody.

106
00:07:42,451 --> 00:07:45,393
A second battery followed in April 2025.

107
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:53,068
Since then, Vietnam: India's Defence Secretary confirmed an order at the Shangri-La Dialogue in May 2026.

108
00:07:53,068 --> 00:08:01,349
And Indonesia signed in July 2026, for a figure reported at between two hundred and three hundred and fifty million dollars.

109
00:08:02,267 --> 00:08:03,644
Who else wants it?

110
00:08:03,644 --> 00:08:10,532
In February 2025, The Tribune reported, citing unnamed sources, that the United Arab Emirates,

111
00:08:10,532 --> 00:08:16,912
Saudi Arabia, Egypt and Vietnam had all shown interest, mostly in a land-based version.

112
00:08:16,912 --> 00:08:19,678
Those are reports, not contracts.

113
00:08:20,567 --> 00:08:22,904
But notice where the actual buyers are.

114
00:08:22,904 --> 00:08:27,345
The Philippines, Vietnam, Indonesia — all three sit on the South China Sea.

115
00:08:27,345 --> 00:08:33,548
A fast anti-ship missile on your own coastline is a very specific answer to a very specific problem.

116
00:08:34,733 --> 00:08:35,702
What's coming?

117
00:08:35,702 --> 00:08:36,476
Two things.

118
00:08:36,476 --> 00:08:41,252
The Lucknow plant, flagged off in October 2025, covers two hundred acres,

119
00:08:41,252 --> 00:08:48,933
cost about three hundred and eighty crore rupees, and is expected to turn out around a hundred missile systems a year.

120
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:51,815
That is a supply line, not a showpiece.

121
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:55,405
And then there's BrahMos-NG — next generation.

122
00:08:55,405 --> 00:09:02,198
Smaller: about six metres instead of eight and a half, and under fifteen hundred kilograms instead of three thousand.

123
00:09:02,198 --> 00:09:09,751
That halving matters, because a lighter missile fits on more aircraft, including the Tejas, and more of them fit on each one.

124
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:19,682
BrahMos Aerospace says the first flight test is expected around 2026, with production from 2027 or 2028.

125
00:09:19,682 --> 00:09:24,139
A hypersonic version, BrahMos-Two, has been talked about for years,

126
00:09:24,139 --> 00:09:29,354
but it remains a concept rather than a programme with hardware in the air.

127
00:09:32,733 --> 00:09:35,807
If you remember one thing, make it three words.

128
00:09:35,807 --> 00:09:40,226
Fast: close to three times the speed of sound, for the whole flight.

129
00:09:40,226 --> 00:09:43,108
Low: down to about ten metres above the sea.

130
00:09:43,108 --> 00:09:47,314
Alone: fire-and-forget, with nobody guiding it after launch.

131
00:09:52,767 --> 00:09:55,742
BrahMos began as a Russian design with an Indian name.

132
00:09:55,742 --> 00:10:02,425
Today it is built in Lucknow, reported to be more than three-quarters Indian by content, and sold to three countries.

133
00:10:06,133 --> 00:10:12,840
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: Agni-Five, and what it means to put many warheads on a single missile.

134
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:16,780
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

